> So, given a neutral priest, not planning on wishing for artifacts, what
> skill choices would you prefer?

As a human you won't get the 'extra arrow per turn using racial
equipment' bonus that the elves can, so don't bother with the bow. The
only ranged attack you can get above basic is with polearms, so you
may want to practice that.

Enhance mace while that is your main weapon. I've had a priest go on
the quest with a +6 mace.

Once you get an artifact of some interest, use that and enhance to
max. (Vorpal Blade, Mjollnir, Magicbane, etc.)

If you get Magicbane, you may want to enchant a stack of daggers to
chuck, but you still won't be able to throw more than one at a time,
so your call whether to bother.

Otherwise, for spells, you'll probably have enough slots for all of
them, though enhancing healing beyond basic is worthless. (At basic
you should be able to cast up to 3. level at 0% failure, and, with a
unihorn, who needs Restore Ability, the 4. level spell?)
Clerical must be enhanced to skilled - skilled remove curse is like
the blessed scroll. Enhance to expert if you want to; Turn Undead is
handy for pets.
Divination - well there are some highlevel spells there which are all
extremely useful. Enhance as much as possible. You have started with
two pretty ok ones; cast Detect Monster as often as you can for
practice; then use Detect Unseen for secret doors and traps when you
have it at a workable failure rate.

After a while, with enough levels and the quest artifact enchanted,
you should be able to cast all but the 5., 6. and 7. level restricted
spells at 0% failure.

Remember to eat. You aren't a wizard and the spellcasting makes you
hungry. I love the priest-class, but I occasionally forget that bit.

